{"artifactHash":"sha256:6ee57ad9dc27cb31ce5d93a86046f60ecce919c9b1933c252c407ce7c26af530","features":["core-executable"],"imports":[],"metadata":{"localSources":{"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_TEXT":"sources/chemistry-2e/en.txt"}},"nodes":[{"anchors":["urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_13_4_ICE"],"body":{"items":[{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#reaction_in_question"},"kind":"status","status":"established"},{"formula":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#mass_balances_presented"},"kind":"status","status":"established"}],"kind":"and"},"contentHash":"sha256:3a2d6b52a48e55c8202acf5c8af0a378b5a707067806141054f6c1efcfb3977a","head":{"args":[{"kind":"var","var":"v0"}],"kind":"literal","polarity":"positive","predicate":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#equilibrium_composition_computable"},"id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#EquilibriumCompositionComputable","interpretations":[],"kind":"rule","labels":[{"language":"en","status":"unofficial","text":"initial amounts and balances: ICE composition (13.4)"},{"language":"ru","status":"unofficial","text":"начальные количества и балансы: состав считается ICE (13.4)"}],"package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","scope":{"kind":"boolean","value":true},"strength":"strict","variables":[{"id":"v0","type":{"name":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#Reaction"}}],"visibility":"public"},{"contentHash":"sha256:fd70862310e1d8472e5cba5c629d12f56d2923fa4d2565e24134e5f438c5ea26","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_2E","jurisdiction":{"kind":"value","type":{"name":"urn:law:std#Text"},"value":"none"},"kind":"source_work","labels":[{"language":"en","status":"official","text":"Chemistry 2e"}],"number":"OpenStax Chemistry 2e (2019), chapter 13","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","sourceKind":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#treatise"},{"contentHash":"sha256:3f1f4474f02ce2311c07460aa8a7b79c462317ca9a514bdb4c1baec208af530c","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","fragmentKind":"paragraph","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_13_2_FORWARD_REVERSE","kind":"fragment","labels":[{"language":"ru","status":"unofficial","text":"13.2: Q < K — нетто-прямая реакция, Q > K — нетто-обратная"}],"locator":"13.2/forward-reverse","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:3f78fde9f566349795b1e38e4e6bc923db6ec1bccc9e3156f0cc7fb3d426a333","language":"en","status":"official","text":"The bar charts in Figure 13.6 represent changes in reactant and product concentrations for three different reaction mixtures. The reaction quotients for mixtures 1 and 3 are initially lesser than the reaction’s equilibrium constant, so each of these mixtures will experience a net forward reaction to achieve equilibrium. The reaction quotient for mixture 2 is initially greater than the equilibrium constant, so this mixture will proceed in the reverse direction until equilibrium is established."}],"visibility":"public"},{"contentHash":"sha256:e9259c1978ae4c8e1a9082d284a485d076e312f67417f9c1faae6dbcef536ae2","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","fragmentKind":"paragraph","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_13_2_MASS_ACTION","kind":"fragment","labels":[{"language":"ru","status":"unofficial","text":"13.2: закон действующих масс — при данной температуре Q в равновесии постоянно"}],"locator":"13.2/mass-action","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:f4dad0ceed6cd966f76fe18932bf871c1e6918d08d98bbc43c49fbc121c403f3","language":"en","status":"official","text":"Comparison of the data plots in Figure 13.5 shows that both experimental scenarios resulted in the same value for the equilibrium constant. This is a general observation for all equilibrium systems, known as the law of mass action: At a given temperature, the reaction quotient for a system at equilibrium is constant."}],"visibility":"public"},{"contentHash":"sha256:75dd1cd9eea0321f8ae30e187a6302cb37959b056b99f81cc3117dda2c1f221a","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","fragmentKind":"paragraph","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_13_2_QK_PREDICTION","kind":"fragment","labels":[{"language":"ru","status":"unofficial","text":"13.2: сравнение Q и K предсказывает направление реакции"}],"locator":"13.2/qk-prediction","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:8b1151bff7f0805c92e721d72fc02fed461ffd36b980bf55ac4aa5f0168ded14","language":"en","status":"official","text":"The equilibrium constant for a reaction can be used to predict the behavior of mixtures containing its reactants and/or products. As demonstrated by the sulfur dioxide oxidation process described above, a chemical reaction will proceed in whatever direction is necessary to achieve equilibrium. Comparing Q to K for an equilibrium system of interest allows prediction of what reaction (forward or reverse), if any, will occur."}],"visibility":"public"},{"contentHash":"sha256:6667eaec42521ab6935452be64f3e82849af050872e9a3e1387af52931e109df","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","fragmentKind":"paragraph","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_13_4_ICE","kind":"fragment","labels":[{"language":"ru","status":"unofficial","text":"13.4: ICE-подход — начальные количества, изменения, равновесный состав"}],"locator":"13.4/ice","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","texts":[{"contentHash":"sha256:9318389bd3243f80e13b4368e3a536a04fef7d855205162d17816f70190e5c34","language":"en","status":"official","text":"The equilibrium constant for a reaction is calculated from the equilibrium concentrations (or pressures) of its reactants and products. If these concentrations are known, the calculation simply involves their substitution into the K expression, as was illustrated by Example 13.2. A slightly more challenging example is provided next, in which the reaction stoichiometry is used to derive equilibrium concentrations from the information provided. The basic strategy of this computation is helpful for many types of equilibrium computations and relies on the use of terms for the reactant and product concentrations initially present, for how they change as the reaction proceeds, and for what they are when the system reaches equilibrium. The acronym ICE is commonly used to refer to this mathematical approach, and the concentrations terms are usually gathered in a tabular format called an ICE table."}],"visibility":"public"},{"contentHash":"sha256:2ebda0cbe05adfa5d816949061ee44f4a07aa04e76945ae047543f77649cbac2","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","kind":"source_edition","labels":[{"language":"en","status":"official","text":"OpenStax Chemistry 2e, English edition (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)"}],"language":"en","lifecycle":[{"extent":"2019-02-14","status":"adopted"},{"extent":{"axis":"legal_time","endInclusive":false,"start":"2019-02-14","startInclusive":true},"status":"in_force"}],"materializationStatus":"PINNED_UNOFFICIAL_COPY","officiality":"official","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","work":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OPENSTAX_CHEMISTRY_2E"},{"contentHash":"sha256:1b3234ce401fb52607a016e945c52157ab9a070531a0fdfcd534cebcbb19976e","documentHash":"sha256:75960d4de233ceb3bbc081d0d3d5930149c60878f1517916fe08c15ee09a3276","edition":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_EN","id":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium#OS_CHEM2E_TEXT","kind":"publication","mediaType":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","package":"urn:openstax:clir:chemistry-equilibrium","retrievedAt":"2026-09-28T00:00:00Z","uri":"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/"},{"contentHash